===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# An ideology of [[masculinity]] or of male rights; ''especially'' an ideology opposed to [[feminism]].
#* '''1996''', Peggy Watson, "The Rise of '''Masculinism''' in Eastern Europe", chapter 6 of Monica Threlfall (editor), ''Mapping the Women’s Movement'', Verso, {{ISBN|978-1-85984-984-2}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=DAak_JlfjkQC&pg=PA216&dq=masculinism page 216]:
#*: {{...}} the transition to liberal capitalism offers men the opportunity of putting a greatly increased social ''distance'' between themselves and women. It is the rise of '''masculinism''' which is the primary characteristic of gender relations in Eastern Europe today.
#* '''2002''', Stephen M. Whitehead, ''Men and Masculinities'', Wiley-Blackwell, {{ISBN|978-0-7456-2467-9}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=UsDAzSPBOBAC&pg=PA115&dq=masculinism page 115]:
#*: Each is laden with assumptions and appropriations that, in discursive terms, reinforce a culture of '''masculinism''' – the cultural dominance of the male (in the public sphere).
#* '''2007''', Satoshi Ikedia, “Masculinity and '''masculinism''' under globalization: Reflections on the Canadian case”, chapter 6 of Marjorie Griffin Cohen and Janine Brodie (editors), ''Remapping Gender in the New Global Order'', Routledge, {{ISBN|978-0-415-76997-6}}, [http://books.google.com/books?id=2EBDWmQ-ZhUC&pg=PA112&dq=masculinism page 112]:
#*: However, it is possible to examine the broader historical contours of '''masculinism''' – the ideology that justifies male domination – and the masculinist institutions that endorse '''masculinism''' {{...}}

